.TH FILE 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
file \- determine file type
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B file
.RB [ \-dh ]
.I file
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B file \-i
.RB [ \-h ]
.I file
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B file
classifies each
.I file
operand and writes one line per operand to standard output, in the
form
\(lq\fIfile\fR: \fItype\fR\(rq
(a symbolic link, without
.BR \-h ,
is instead reported as
\(lq\fIfile\fR: symbolic link to \fItarget\fR\(rq).
Classification proceeds through a fixed set of checks, cheapest first:
the file's type as reported by the filesystem (directory, FIFO,
socket, block or character special file, symbolic link, or regular
file), and, for a non-empty regular file only, a best-effort look at
its first few kilobytes of content.
.PP
The content check recognizes:
.TP
.B executable
An ELF (\(lq\fB\ex7fELF\fR\(rq) or PE/DOS (\(lq\fBMZ\fR\(rq) header at
the start of the file.
.TP
.B archive
The classic archive magic
.RB \(lq !<arch>\en \(rq
at the start of the file.
.TP
.B "tar archive"
The ustar magic
.RB \(lq ustar \(rq
at byte offset 257.
.TP
.B "cpio archive"
The octet-oriented cpio magic
.RB \(lq 070707 \(rq
at the start of the file.
.TP
.B "commands text"
A
.RB \(lq #! \(rq
shebang at the start of the file.
.TP
.B "ASCII text"
Every byte examined is a tab, carriage return, line feed, or
printable 7-bit ASCII byte.
.TP
.B data
None of the above; in particular, any byte outside that printable/
whitespace set, including a NUL byte or a non-ASCII byte with no
recognized magic. This build does not attempt to distinguish, for
example, C or Fortran source text from other plain text, or to
identify any binary format beyond the ones listed above; both are
reported as
.B "ASCII text"
or
.B data
respectively.
.PP
An empty regular file is reported as
.BR empty ,
and a file that cannot be opened or stat'd is reported as
.BR "cannot open" ,
without attempting any content check.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-d
Apply the default classification described above. This is already
the default when neither
.B \-M
nor
.B \-m
is given, so
.B \-d
has no additional effect.
.TP
.B \-h
Do not resolve a symbolic link
.I file
operand to what it points to; report it as a symbolic link, and its
raw target text, even when the target exists. Without
.BR \-h ,
a symbolic link whose target exists is classified by the referent
(though the operand's own name is still what is printed); a symbolic
link whose target does not exist is always reported as a symbolic
link regardless of
.BR \-h .
.TP
.B \-i
Do not attempt to classify a regular file's content at all: every
regular file, empty or not, is reported simply as
.RB \(lq "regular file" \(rq .
Every other file type is still classified as usual.
.TP
.BI \-M " file"
Not implemented (supply the set of content tests from a custom
test-file description, replacing the built-in set). Refused with a
diagnostic.
.TP
.BI \-m " file"
Not implemented (extend the built-in set of content tests with a
custom test-file description). Refused with a diagnostic; only the
built-in test set above is available.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
A pathname to classify. A single
.RB \(lq \- \(rq
operand is classified from standard input itself (via its open file
description, since there is no seekable path to reopen), rather than
being taken as a literal filename.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every operand was classified successfully.
.TP
.B 1
One or more operands could not be opened or stat'd.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error occurred: an invalid option, or
.B \-M
or
.B \-m
was given.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ls (1),
.BR nm (1)
